results.abstract:

Marxists have different ways of separating materialism from Hegelian idealism. They might, for example, refer to the independent existence of matter as opposed to ideas. However, dialectical materialism implies a social theory where forces are much more than material. They involve social laws and relations. At this point, the space between Hegelian idealism and Marxist materialism shrinks. I draw on Hegel and Marx to argue that the Japanese philosopher Miki Kiyoshi tried to construct a perspective beyond the opposition between ideas and matter. In the 1920s and 1930s, Miki drew on Marx’s analysis of the commodity to construct a theory that questioned conventional understandings of materialism. He eventually combines this Marxist perspective with Heidegger’s idea of basic experience (
Grunderfahrung
). Perhaps because of the Heideggerian legacy, there are ways in which Miki and certain versions of Marxism develop a theory that are materialist in a more practical sense than most uses of the term.
